question.:question/problem:
Find a symmetric Cartesian equation of the normal line to the surface

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
&\left(2 - z\right)\\
&=x^{2}\\
&{}-\left(y + 2\right)^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

at $P(2,-1,-1)$.

Find a symmetric Cartesian equation of the normal line to the surface

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
&\left(y + 2\right)\\
&=\ln{\left(2 \left(2 - z\right) + x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

at $P(-1,-2,1)$.

question.:question/worked-solution:
Bring the variable terms together and set

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F(x,y,z)&=\sqrt{2 \left(2 - z\right)^{2} + x - 4 \left(y + 2\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

The surface is the level surface $F=1$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(x,y,z)&=\frac{1}{2 \sqrt{2 \left(2 - z\right)^{2} + x - 4 \left(y + 2\right)}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(x,y,z)&=-\frac{2}{\sqrt{2 \left(2 - z\right)^{2} + x - 4 \left(y + 2\right)}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(x,y,z)&=-\frac{2 \left(2 - z\right)}{\sqrt{2 \left(2 - z\right)^{2} + x - 4 \left(y + 2\right)}}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
\left(2 - z\right)&=2\\
x&=1\\
\left(y + 2\right)&=2
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these values into the partial derivatives:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(P)&=\frac{1}{2 \sqrt{2 \left(2\right)^{2} + \left(1\right) - 4 \left(2\right)}}\\
&=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(P)&=-\frac{2}{\sqrt{2 \left(2\right)^{2} + \left(1\right) - 4 \left(2\right)}}\\
&=-2
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(P)&=-\frac{2 \left(2\right)}{\sqrt{2 \left(2\right)^{2} + \left(1\right) - 4 \left(2\right)}}\\
&=-4
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\nabla F(1,0,0)=\langle \frac{1}{2},-2,-4\rangle\ne\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.

Multiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $-1$ to obtain the equivalent direction

$$
\mathbf n=-1\langle \frac{1}{2},-2,-4\rangle=\langle - \frac{1}{2},2,4\rangle.
$$

The point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:

$$
\mathbf r=\langle 1,0,0\rangle+t\,\langle - \frac{1}{2},2,4\rangle,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.
$$

The coordinate equations are

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
x&=1 - \frac{t}{2}\\
y&=2 t\\
z&=4 t
\end{aligned}
$$

Solve each for $t$ and equate the ratios:

$$
\frac{x - 1}{- \frac{1}{2}}=\frac{y}{2}=\frac{z}{4}.
$$

question.:question/problem:
Find a symmetric Cartesian equation of the normal line to the surface

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
&\left(y + 2\right)\\
&=x^{2}\\
&{}+\sin{\left(\left(2 - z\right) x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

at $P(-1,-1,2)$.

Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
&-x^{2}\\
&{}+\frac{\left(2 - z\right)^{2}}{4}\\
&=\left(y + 2\right)^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

at $P(0,-1,0)$. Use $t\in\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$.

question.:question/worked-solution:
Bring the variable terms together and set

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F(x,y,z)&=-x^{2}\\
&{}-\left(y + 2\right)^{2}\\
&{}+\frac{\left(2 - z\right)^{2}}{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

The surface is the level surface $F=0$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(x,y,z)&=-2 x
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(x,y,z)&=-2 \left(y + 2\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(x,y,z)&=-\frac{\left(2 - z\right)}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
\left(2 - z\right)&=2\\
x&=0\\
\left(y + 2\right)&=1
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these values into the partial derivatives:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(P)&=-2 \left(0\right)\\
&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(P)&=-2 \left(1\right)\\
&=-2
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(P)&=-\frac{\left(2\right)}{2}\\
&=-1
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\nabla F(0,-1,0)=\langle 0,-2,-1\rangle\ne\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.

Multiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $2$ to obtain the equivalent direction

$$
\mathbf n=2\langle 0,-2,-1\rangle=\langle 0,-4,-2\rangle.
$$

The point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:

$$
\mathbf r=\langle 0,-1,0\rangle+t\,\langle 0,-4,-2\rangle,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.
$$

Bring the variable terms together and set

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F(x,y,z)&=-1\\
&{}+\left(2 - z\right)^{2}\\
&{}+\frac{\left(y + 2\right)^{2}}{4}\\
&{}+\frac{x^{2}}{9}
\end{aligned}
$$

The surface is the level surface $F=0$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(x,y,z)&=\frac{2 x}{9}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(x,y,z)&=\frac{\left(y + 2\right)}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(x,y,z)&=-2 \left(2 - z\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
\left(2 - z\right)&=0\\
x&=0\\
\left(y + 2\right)&=2
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these values into the partial derivatives:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(P)&=\frac{2 \left(0\right)}{9}\\
&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(P)&=\frac{\left(2\right)}{2}\\
&=1
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(P)&=-2 \left(0\right)\\
&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\nabla F(0,0,2)=\langle 0,1,0\rangle\ne\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.

Multiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction

$$
\mathbf n=\frac{1}{2}\langle 0,1,0\rangle=\langle 0,\frac{1}{2},0\rangle.
$$

The point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:

$$
\mathbf r=\langle 0,0,2\rangle+t\,\langle 0,\frac{1}{2},0\rangle,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.
$$

Bring the variable terms together and set

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F(x,y,z)&=\ln{\left(\left(2 - z\right)^{2} + x^{2} + \left(y + 2\right)^{2} \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

The surface is the level surface $F=1$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(x,y,z)&=\frac{2 x}{\left(2 - z\right)^{2} + x^{2} + \left(y + 2\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(x,y,z)&=\frac{2 \left(y + 2\right)}{\left(2 - z\right)^{2} + x^{2} + \left(y + 2\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(x,y,z)&=-\frac{2 \left(2 - z\right)}{\left(2 - z\right)^{2} + x^{2} + \left(y + 2\right)^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
\left(2 - z\right)&=0\\
x&=0\\
\left(y + 2\right)&=e^{\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these values into the partial derivatives:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(P)&=\frac{2 \left(0\right)}{\left(0\right)^{2} + \left(0\right)^{2} + \left(e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)^{2}}\\
&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(P)&=\frac{2 \left(e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)}{\left(0\right)^{2} + \left(0\right)^{2} + \left(e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)^{2}}\\
&=\frac{2}{e^{\frac{1}{2}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(P)&=-\frac{2 \left(0\right)}{\left(0\right)^{2} + \left(0\right)^{2} + \left(e^{\frac{1}{2}}\right)^{2}}\\
&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\nabla F(0,-2 + e^{\frac{1}{2}},2)=\langle 0,\frac{2}{e^{\frac{1}{2}}},0\rangle\ne\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.

Multiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction

$$
\mathbf n=\frac{1}{2}\langle 0,\frac{2}{e^{\frac{1}{2}}},0\rangle=\langle 0,e^{- \frac{1}{2}},0\rangle.
$$

The point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:

$$
\mathbf r=\langle 0,-2 + e^{\frac{1}{2}},2\rangle+t\,\langle 0,e^{- \frac{1}{2}},0\rangle,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.
$$

Equating coordinates gives

$$
\left\{\begin{aligned}x&=0\\y&=\frac{t}{e^{\frac{1}{2}}} - 2 + e^{\frac{1}{2}}\\z&=2\end{aligned}\right..
$$

question.:question/problem:
Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
&\ln{\left(\left(2 - z\right) + x + \left(y + 2\right) \right)}\\
&=-\left(2 - z\right) x \left(y + 2\right)\\
&{}+\ln{\left(2 \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

at $P(0,-1,1)$. Use $t\in\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$.

question.:question/worked-solution:
Bring the variable terms together and set

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F(x,y,z)&=\left(2 - z\right) x \left(y + 2\right)\\
&{}+\ln{\left(\left(2 - z\right) + x + \left(y + 2\right) \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

The surface is the level surface $F=\ln{\left(2 \right)}$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(x,y,z)&=\frac{1}{\left(2 - z\right) + x + \left(y + 2\right)}\\
&{}+\left(2 - z\right) \left(y + 2\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(x,y,z)&=\frac{1}{\left(2 - z\right) + x + \left(y + 2\right)}\\
&{}+\left(2 - z\right) x
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(x,y,z)&=-\frac{1}{\left(2 - z\right) + x + \left(y + 2\right)}\\
&{}-x \left(y + 2\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
\left(2 - z\right)&=1\\
x&=0\\
\left(y + 2\right)&=1
\end{aligned}
$$

Substitute these values into the partial derivatives:

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{x}(P)&=\frac{1}{\left(1\right) + \left(0\right) + \left(1\right)}\\
&{}+\left(1\right) \left(1\right)\\
&=\frac{3}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{y}(P)&=\frac{1}{\left(1\right) + \left(0\right) + \left(1\right)}\\
&{}+\left(1\right) \left(0\right)\\
&=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
F_{z}(P)&=-\frac{1}{\left(1\right) + \left(0\right) + \left(1\right)}\\
&{}-\left(0\right) \left(1\right)\\
&=- \frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus $\nabla F(0,-1,1)=\langle \frac{3}{2},\frac{1}{2},- \frac{1}{2}\rangle\ne\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.

Multiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $2$ to obtain the equivalent direction

$$
\mathbf n=2\langle \frac{3}{2},\frac{1}{2},- \frac{1}{2}\rangle=\langle 3,1,-1\rangle.
$$

The point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:

$$
\mathbf r=\langle 0,-1,1\rangle+t\,\langle 3,1,-1\rangle,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.
$$

question.:question/problem:
Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface

$$
\small\begin{aligned}
&\left(y + 2\right)\\
&=\left(2 - z\right)\\
&{}+2 \left(2 - z\right)^{2}\\
&{}+2 x^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

at $P(1,1,\frac{3}{2})$. Use $t\in\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$.
